      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>Mosafir is a travel platform built for travel operators and destination-focused tourism businesses. The product was designed to replace fragmented inquiry-based booking processes with a faster, self-serve travel experience.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to support seasonal demand spikes, high-intent search traffic, and operator-side reservation coordination without performance regressions. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve online travel and tourism use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The client needed a booking engine that improved conversion for travelers while also giving internal teams much better visibility into reservations, payment state, and operational follow-up.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Inventory Accuracy</h3>
              <p>Travel packages, dates, and seat availability needed dependable synchronization across traveler and operator actions.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Payment Confirmation</h3>
              <p>Bookings could not drift into ambiguous states after redirects, retries, or delayed gateway callbacks.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Search Performance</h3>
              <p>Destination and package browsing had to stay fast as the content catalog expanded.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Support Visibility</h3>
              <p>Operations teams needed booking timelines, traveler details, and change history without engineering handoffs.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel 12, Blade, Vue 2, OpenAI, JWT, REST APIs, Payfast, WebSockets, Redis, MySQL, Azure CI/CD across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">Traveler UI</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Booking Services</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">Payments</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Operator Console</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">MySQL + Redis</div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

