              <h3>Payments & Booking Security</h3>
              <p>Integrated payment callbacks, booking state rules, and transaction visibility for safe checkouts.</p>
              <span>Improved payment confidence and reduced manual reconciliation.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-broadcast"></i>
              <h3>Realtime Operator Updates</h3>
              <p>Delivered booking changes and traveler actions to internal dashboards with event-driven updates.</p>
              <span>Helped teams respond faster to time-sensitive trip changes.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="service-card">
              <i class="bi bi-stars"></i>
              <h3>AI-Assisted Content Operations</h3>
              <p>Used AI support for package copy enrichment and destination content workflows.</p>
              <span>Improved merchandising quality without slowing content teams.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-deep">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Performance &amp; Scalability</span>
              <h2>Technical decisions that improved stability and maintainability</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Architecture choices focused on safe growth, cleaner releases, and operational clarity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Asynchronous workflow handling</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The platform had to handle seasonal demand spikes, high-intent search traffic, and operator-side reservation coordination without performance regressions.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> This added queue, retry, and observability responsibilities to the architecture.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> Heavy tasks moved out of request-response flows, keeping the product faster and more stable.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Service-layer integration boundaries</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The product relied on multiple external or subsystem workflows across online travel and tourism.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> More explicit service boundaries required extra design effort early in delivery.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> The codebase became safer to extend and easier to debug as the platform evolved.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <h3>Domain-first product modeling</h3>
              <p><strong>Why chosen:</strong> The business needed stronger operational clarity around replace fragmented inquiry-based booking processes with a faster, self-serve travel experience.</p>
              <p><strong>Tradeoff:</strong> It took longer to model states and rules than a quick CRUD implementation would have.</p>
              <span class="feature-meta"><strong>Outcome:</strong> The result was a system with clearer workflows, better reporting, and lower long-term maintenance risk.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

